B. Why is the warm-up call required?
@njit compiles the function only during the first call. This first call includes type checking and generating machine code with LLVM, which takes extra time and is not part of the algorithm.
Without the warm-up, this compilation time would be included in the first measured run (1-thread baseline). This would make T₁ too large and the speedup look higher than it really is.
The warm-up call with a small input (10_000) compiles the function and starts the thread pool. After that, the benchmark measures only the actual execution time.
C. Did efficiency stay at 100%?
No. Efficiency was close to 100% from 1 to 4 threads (99.1% at 4 threads). At 8 threads it dropped to 55.9%, and at 12 threads it was 54.7%. The speedup at 12 threads was 6.57x instead of the ideal 12x.
Amdahl's Law is not the main reason. The serial part is only about 0.3%, so Amdahl's Law predicts about 11.6x speedup with 12 threads. The actual speedup is 6.57x, so the main reason is related to the CPU hardware.
Physical cores and SMT. The Ryzen 5 4600H has 6 physical cores and 12 logical threads. Performance is almost ideal up to 4 threads. After 6 threads, multiple threads have to share the same physical cores using SMT. A second thread on a core does not give the same performance as a new physical core. This helps explain why the speedup at 12 threads is only 6.57x.
Memory contention. This benchmark does not use arrays, so memory contention is not an important factor here.
CPU frequency. When all CPU cores are working, a laptop CPU can reduce its clock speed because of power and temperature limits. This can reduce performance. CPU frequency was not measured, so this is only a possible factor.
Parallel overhead. Thread creation and synchronization add some overhead, but it is small compared with the total runtime of about 2 seconds.
D. Why does inside_circle += 1 not corrupt the count?
Numba treats inside_circle += 1 inside prange as a reduction operation.
Each thread has its own copy of inside_circle, starting from 0. The threads update their own copies, so they do not write to the same memory at the same time. Therefore, there is no race condition.
At the end of the loop, Numba adds all the partial results together to get the final count.
This works similarly to the OpenMP reduction(+:inside_circle) clause.

