A. Compare t_rows vs t_cols.
On my machine, the column version was slightly faster: t_cols = 0.842 s, while t_rows = 0.891 s. This is about 5.8% faster, with a ratio of 1.06.
The difference is small because each version was tested only once, so this result is not very strong. Normally, the row version should be faster because NumPy uses row-major order. However, the measured results do not show a clear disadvantage for the column version.
One possible reason is that most of the time is spent on calculations, not memory writes. Each pixel can require up to 1000 iterations, but only one 4-byte value is written. Another possible reason is that the two versions distribute the more difficult pixels differently between threads. However, I did not measure the workload of each thread, so this is only a possible explanation.
B. C-contiguous layout and cache lines.
NumPy arrays use C-contiguous (row-major) layout. This means that img[r, c] and img[r, c+1] are next to each other in memory. One row is stored as one continuous block of memory.
A cache line is usually 64 bytes, so it can contain 16 int32 values.
In the row version, each thread writes complete rows one after another. This gives good memory locality, and different threads mostly work with different parts of memory. Cache lines can be shared only near the boundaries between row blocks.
In the column version, a thread writes img[r, c] for different values of r. The distance between two writes is w × 4 = 10,000 bytes. This means that almost every write uses a different cache line, so memory locality is worse.
Also, neighbouring columns can be processed by different threads and can belong to the same cache line. This can cause false sharing, where cache lines are repeatedly transferred between CPU cores. With continuous blocks of columns, this mainly happens near the boundaries between thread blocks.
However, in this lab, the calculations for each pixel take much more time than the memory write, so the effect is small.
C. Load imbalance and dynamic scheduling.
Mandelbrot pixels can require very different amounts of work. Some points leave the set after only a few iterations, while points inside the set can require all 1000 iterations.
The top 20% of rows, given to thread 0, are mostly outside the set, so they finish quickly. The center 20% of rows, given to thread 2, contain much more of the set, so many pixels require all 1000 iterations.
With the default static scheduling, each thread gets a fixed block of rows. Thread 0 can finish early and then wait at the end of the loop while thread 2 is still working. The total time depends on the slowest thread, so some CPU time is wasted.
With OpenMP dynamic scheduling (schedule(dynamic, chunk)), the rows are divided into smaller chunks. When a thread finishes its current chunk, it gets another one. Threads that finish easy rows can therefore continue working on other chunks. This keeps the threads busy for longer.
The disadvantage is a small extra overhead because the threads need to get new chunks during execution.
