A. Benchmark runtime and throughput (float64).
Runtime was 1.004 s and throughput was 672.30 Megacells/sec. This was measured with 12 threads after the JIT warm-up.
B. Effect of float64 → float32.
The runtime decreased from 1.004 s to 0.509 s. This is a 1.97x speedup. Throughput increased from 672.30 to 1324.84 Megacells/sec.
Why does it help so much? Each grid has 1500 × 1500 elements. Two grids use about 36 MB with float64 and about 18 MB with float32. The CPU has only 8 MB of L3 cache, so the grids do not fit in the cache. The program therefore needs to load the data from main memory at every time step.
The stencil does not perform many calculations for each cell. Because of this, the program spends a lot of time moving data rather than doing calculations. Using float32 cuts the data size in half, so less data needs to be moved. Also, a 64-byte cache line can hold 16 float32 values instead of 8 float64 values.
Why is it not mainly about computation? The number of calculations per cell stays the same for both data types. The measured speedup is 1.97x, which is almost exactly 2x. This matches the reduction in data size and suggests that the program is mainly limited by memory speed. I did not measure SIMD performance separately, so I cannot say how much of the improvement comes from vectorization.
C. Memory Roofline Model.
The Roofline model says that performance is limited by either computing power or memory bandwidth. It can be written as P = min(P_peak, I × B), where P_peak is the maximum computing performance, B is memory bandwidth, and I is the number of floating-point operations per byte of data moved from memory.
The 5-point stencil performs about 7 floating-point operations per cell but needs at least 16 bytes of data movement per cell in float64. This gives a low arithmetic intensity, so the program is mainly limited by memory bandwidth rather than CPU computing power.
Adding more CPU cores increases the available computing power, but all cores still share the same memory system. Once the memory bandwidth is fully used, additional cores have to wait for data. Because of this, doubling the number of cores does not necessarily give 2x better performance.
Performance can be improved by moving less data, for example by using float32, or by reusing data in the cache.
In this lab, I measured thread scaling only for Challenge 1, not for the stencil. Therefore, the explanation about stencil scaling is based on the Roofline model, not on my own thread-scaling measurements.

